Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:07.580
JEN PERSON: Give me
one minute and I'll

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.850
show you how Filestore can help
you manage your file system.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.640
Filestore is a managed file
storage service for apps

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.160
that require a file system
interface and a shared file

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.870
system for data.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.180
It gives users a simple,
native experience

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.730
for standing up managed
network-attached storage

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.730
with their VM instances in
Compute Engine and Google

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.320
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.570
Filestore offers low
latency for file operations

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.560
so it's perfect for workloads
like data analytics, media

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.220
rendering, genomics processing,
or other media-intensive

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.180
application.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.150
You can pick a performance
tier and the storage capacity

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.980
you need with
Filestore, which enables

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.860
you to tune your file system
for a particular workload.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.280
Use Cloud Console, the
API, or simple gcloud

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.490
commands to create a
Filestore instance.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.440
Applications across
your organization

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.890
probably need to access
and share common datasets.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.960
You can host file
content in Filestore

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.840
and enable shared
access to that data.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.600
Filestore helps creators, like
graphic designers and video

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.360
editors, access shared storage
to manipulate and produce

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.950
large files.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.800
So next time you're looking
to manage files, take a minute

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.240
to look at Filestore.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.724
To learn more, check out
cloud.google.com/filestore.

